tary leaders & commanders.", "newValue": "Marcus Tullius Cicero Minor (b. 64 BCE, Rome), son of the revered orator Cicero, navigated the tumultuous transition from the Roman Republic to the Augustan Principate. Though often overshadowed by his father's monumental legacy, Cicero Minor carved out a distinguished public career as a military officer and civil administrator, serving under key figures like Brutus and later Augustus himself. His life trajectory offers a unique lens into the political and military upheavals that defined the late Classical era, embodying the challenges faced by the Roman senatorial elite during a period of civil war and profound constitutional change.

Educated in philosophy in Athens, Cicero Minor initially sided with the Optimates, fighting alongside Pompey the Great's forces against Julius Caesar. Following Caesar's assassination in 44 BCE, he joined the Liberators, serving as a tribune in Brutus's army and participating in the Battle of Philippi in 42 BCE, where he commanded a legionary cavalry detachment. Despite the defeat, he was later pardoned by Octavian (the future Augustus) and reintegrated into Roman public life, serving as quaestor in 43 BCE and eventually achieving the prestigious position of Augur.

